WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.022
Many integer bugs appear during conversion
rather than during arithmetic.

2
00:00:04.102 --> 00:00:06.468
A value may be parsed as one type, stored
as another,

3
00:00:06.548 --> 00:00:10.236
sent over a protocol as a fixed-width
field, converted to `usize` for indexing,

4
00:00:10.316 --> 00:00:12.792
or cast across signed and unsigned
representations.

5
00:00:12.872 --> 00:00:15.128
Each transition is a chance for meaning to
drift.

6
00:00:15.208 --> 00:00:19.171
Widening conversions are usually easier to
reason about than narrowing conversions

7
00:00:19.251 --> 00:00:21.617
because the target type can represent more
values.

8
00:00:21.697 --> 00:00:25.494
Narrowing conversions require review
because the target may not represent every

9
00:00:25.574 --> 00:00:26.510
valid source value.

10
00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:30.222
Signedness changes also need attention
because a value that means one thing as

11
00:00:30.302 --> 00:00:33.274
signed data may mean something very
different as unsigned data.

12
00:00:33.354 --> 00:00:36.380
The `as` operator can be concise, but it
can also hide truncation,

13
00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.992
reinterpretation, or unexpected boundary
behavior.

14
00:00:39.072 --> 00:00:41.053
That does not mean every `as` cast is
wrong.

15
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:42.894
It means cast sites are review points.

16
00:00:42.974 --> 00:00:46.111
The reviewer should understand the source
range, the target range,

17
00:00:46.191 --> 00:00:48.722
and why the conversion preserves the
value's meaning.

18
00:00:48.802 --> 00:00:52.655
Fallible conversion patterns such as
`TryFrom` make boundary failures explicit.

19
00:00:52.735 --> 00:00:55.266
Instead of silently forcing a value into a
target type,

20
00:00:55.346 --> 00:00:58.373
the code can return an error, reject the
input, choose a fallback,

21
00:00:58.453 --> 00:00:59.829
or apply a documented policy.

22
00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:03.101
That is especially useful at parser, FFI,
serialization, indexing,

23
00:01:03.181 --> 00:01:04.501
and allocation boundaries.

24
00:01:04.581 --> 00:01:07.058
Defensive Rust validates ranges before
conversion.

25
00:01:07.138 --> 00:01:10.495
If a parsed value will become an index, a
length, a retry count, a timeout,

26
00:01:10.575 --> 00:01:14.042
a port, or a protocol field, validate the
allowed range in the domain first.

27
00:01:14.122 --> 00:01:16.598
Conversion should preserve a decision
already made,

28
00:01:16.678 --> 00:01:19.540
not smuggle an unchecked value into a more
dangerous context.
